<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Gilbert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1544">1544</a>–<a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Gilberd</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> was an English physician, physicist and natural philosopher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a> who rejected Aristotelian philosophy and the Scholastic method of university teaching. Educated at St John's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, he practised medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, became President of the Royal College of Physicians in 1600 and was physician to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> from 1601 until her death in 1603. His book De Magnete, published in 1600, described experiments with a model Earth, the terrella, and concluded that the Earth itself was magnetic, which is why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compasses</a> point north. His Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word electricus, meaning like amber, gave English the word electricity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-gilbert-physicist.jpg" alt="William Gilbert" width="330" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Gilbert_45626i.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
